WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.054
Yeah, let's shift to dynamic thumbnails. I'm really excited

00:00:03.054 --> 00:00:05.952
about this, partially because I love how YouTube sort of

00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:08.540
people like you figure out how to hack the system.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.187
Like you use either end cards to A-B test or some people use

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:14.440
like like promotion to A-B test videos.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.294
Then YouTube is like, oh, people are doing this anyway.

00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:18.940
We'll do A-B testing for for videos.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.092
And then some people are like, well, I'm going to A-B test

00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:25.000
in the beginning because my my regular audience likes my

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:25.260
face.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.578
But later on, I'm going to switch to like a device picture

00:00:28.578 --> 00:00:32.011
because my cold like my new audience doesn't know me or like

00:00:32.011 --> 00:00:35.100
I have like a romance and adventure and a comedy plot.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.682
I'm going to A-B test these different things to see which of

00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:38.740
those audiences is bigger.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.166
Now you have the opportunity to actually create four

00:00:42.166 --> 00:00:44.260
different audiences, three thumbnails.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.441
It will run the test and you can you can sort of target

00:00:47.441 --> 00:00:48.740
different audiences with them.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.200
Yeah, I mean, that's extremely exciting.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.198
Sometimes like, for example, when we released the 100 cops

00:00:54.198 --> 00:00:57.195
video, we noticed that there was huge amount of, you know,

00:00:57.195 --> 00:01:00.038
search traffic for the video because it was been it was

00:01:00.038 --> 00:01:01.640
clipped across multiple places.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.980
And people were really interested in this explosion.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.691
We didn't have the explosion in the thumbnail, but there's

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:09.080
viewers out there that would have enjoyed the explosions.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.941
There's viewers out there that wouldn't have enjoyed the

00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:12.440
explosions.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.424
So we just do two different thumbnails and, you know, it

00:01:15.424 --> 00:01:16.280
finds the viewers.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.780
I have so many questions on dynamic thumbnails.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.660
So let's bring it. I'm going to ask you a bunch.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.786
Like, how do you determine, you know, what thumbnail is

00:01:23.786 --> 00:01:24.980
shown to what person?

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.172
Right. So, like I said, there's some randomness involved

00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:32.514
when we get started because we don't really know what works

00:01:32.514 --> 00:01:33.760
for for which segment.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:37.190
But then we have a lot of different what we might call

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:38.200
segment features.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.500
They could be like, what is the location of this person?

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.671
It could be something, you know, one of the ones I'm most

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:48.080
excited about us testing is how much.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.820
Has this viewer watched this channel before?

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.880
Are they new to the channel or are they, you know, a core

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.220
fan?

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:01.397
And, you know, we're going to be iterating on this with

00:02:01.397 --> 00:02:02.080
creators.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.036
So I'm eager. I'm going to ask you as well. You know, what

00:02:05.036 --> 00:02:07.380
do you think the different segments should be?
